{"answer":"As Rina, I understand that ‘share the surplus with the community’ means that if I pull more than the 1.5‑unit limit, I must immediately set aside the extra fish, leave it on the shore in the community pouch, and let the next villager take it up to the 1.5‑unit cap. The record‑keeper records the amount I caught (up to 1.5), the surplus I set aside, and the time of my departure and return. If I fail to do this and keep the extra fish, the violation is posted on the tally board, I am publicly warned, and I must skip my next trip as punishment. This keeps the lake fair for everyone and ensures the surplus is returned to the community stock_kg for shared use.

I restate my proposal exactly as we discussed:
"Each villager may take only one fishing trip per day and may not exceed a catch of 1.5 units of fish. If a trip yields more than 1.5 units the excess must be returned to the lake for community use. A small marked pouch (the community pouch) holds the maximum 1.5 units per trip. When a fisher pulls more than the pouch allows, they must immediately set aside the surplus and leave it on the shore for the next villager to use. A simple tally board will note each person’s daily catch and any surplus returned. Violations will be publicly noted and the offending fisher will be asked to share their surplus with the community or skip a trip for the next day. The record‑keeper updates the tally board each day, records any surplus and any violations. When a violation is noted, the record‑keeper publicly posts the violation on the board and informs the fisher that they must skip their next trip. The record‑keeper checks that the fisher indeed forgoes the trip before the following day’s catch. Any surplus fish is added to the community stock_kg and made available for the next villager to take up to the 1.5‑unit limit. — The record‑keeper, assisted by a rotating watch of trusted villagers, will observe each fisher’s departure and return times. Each fisher will sign the tally board with their name, the time they set out, the time they return, and the amount of fish taken (up to 1.5 units). The record‑keeper will verify that the amount in the pouch does not exceed 1.5 units and will record any surplus on the board for the next fisher. If a fisher attempts a second departure, the record‑keeper will record a violation, publicly post it on the board, and instruct the fisher to skip the next day’s trip. The other fishers will be encouraged to remind the record‑keeper of any double departures, ensuring the rule is followed by the community as a whole."","revised_policy":"Each villager may take only one fishing trip per day and may not exceed a catch of 1.5 units of fish. If a trip yields more than 1.5 units the excess must be returned to the lake for community use. A small marked pouch (the community pouch) holds the maximum 1.5 units per trip. When a fisher pulls more than the pouch allows, they must immediately set aside the surplus and leave it on the shore for the next villager to use. A simple tally board will note each person’s daily catch and any surplus returned. Violations will be publicly noted and the offending fisher will be asked to share their surplus with the community or skip a trip for the next day. The record‑keeper updates the tally board each day, records any surplus and any violations. When a violation is noted, the record‑keeper publicly posts the violation on the board and informs the fisher that they must skip their next trip. The record‑keeper checks that the fisher indeed forgoes the trip before the following day’s catch. Any surplus fish is added to the community stock_kg and made available for the next villager to take up to the 1.5‑unit limit. — The record‑keeper, assisted by a rotating watch of trusted villagers, will observe each fisher’s departure and return times. Each fisher will sign the tally board with their name, the time they set out, the time they return, and the amount of fish taken (up to 1.5 units). The record‑keeper will verify that the amount in the pouch does not exceed 1.5 units and will record any surplus on the board for the next fisher. If a fisher attempts a second departure, the record‑keeper will record a violation, publicly post it on the board, and instruct the fisher to skip the next day’s trip. The other fishers will be encouraged to remind the record‑keeper of any double departures, ensuring the rule is followed by the community as a whole.","revised_operationalization":"unchanged","reasoning":"I kept the policy unchanged because the question was about clarifying the meaning of ‘share the surplus’ and its enforcement. I explained that sharing means handing the excess to the community pouch, with the tally board recording the action, and violations leading to a public post and a skip of the next trip, which aligns with the original operational rules."}

